        model_answer:
          "The standard of hospital care and nursing improved significantly in the second half of the nineteenth century primarily due to Florence Nightingale’s sanitary reforms during the Crimean War, the architectural revolution of the pavilion hospital design, and the institutional professionalisation of nursing.<br><br>A primary catalyst for improvement was Florence Nightingale’s work at the Scutari Barrack Hospital during the Crimean War (1854–1856). Before 1854, British hospitals were notorious 'death houses' where patients were packed into unventilated wards on dirty bedding, and mortality from 'hospitalism' (gangrene and pyaemia) was catastrophic. Arriving in Scutari, Nightingale found 4,000 wounded soldiers lying in filthy rags above blocked sewers. Undeterred by military obstruction, Nightingale instituted ruthless hygiene: scrubbing floors with boiling water, purchasing clean linen, opening windows for cross-ventilation, and opening kitchens to serve nutritious meals. Crucially, when a government Sanitary Commission flushed the underground sewers, the Scutari mortality rate dropped from 42% to just 2%. Nightingale proved that hospital deaths were not inevitable consequences of illness, but the direct result of environmental filth.<br><br>Building on this breakthrough, hospital standards improved dramatically through the widespread adoption of Nightingale’s 'Pavilion Plan' in hospital architecture. In her influential 1863 treatise Notes on Hospitals, Nightingale used statistical evidence to demonstrate that overcrowded, dark wards concentrated poisonous miasma and disease. She established the pavilion model: constructing hospitals as separate, detached wings connected by covered corridors. Each ward was designed to be long and narrow with large facing windows to ensure constant fresh-air cross-ventilation, ceilings were high, surfaces were lined with wipeable glazed tiles rather than porous plaster, and beds were spaced strictly eight feet apart. When St Thomas’ Hospital in London was rebuilt in 1871, it followed the pavilion plan. Consequently, cross-infection between patients was drastically reduced, transforming hospitals from death traps into safe clinical environments.<br><br>Furthermore, nursing was fundamentally transformed from an uneducated servant chore into a respected, disciplined profession. Before 1860, nurses were poorly paid, illiterate, and stereotyped as drunken, negligent drudges typified by Charles Dickens’s character Mrs Gamp. In 1860, using £44,000 raised by public subscription, Nightingale founded the Nightingale Training School for Nurses at St Thomas’ Hospital. The school introduced a rigorous, science-based curriculum covering anatomy, wound hygiene, and patient observation, enforcing strict moral codes and professional uniforms. Matrons trained at St Thomas’ were appointed to lead hospitals across Britain and the Empire. This directly resulted in hospitals being staffed by trained, skilled medical professionals rather than casual domestic labourers, cementing permanent improvements in patient survival.",
