        model_answer:
          "Surgery became significantly safer in the period c1840–c1900 primarily due to the discovery of effective anaesthetics which conquered pain and shock, the development of antiseptic techniques which prevented fatal wound infections, and the subsequent transition to aseptic operating theatres.<br><br>A primary reason surgery became safer was the conquest of pain through James Young Simpson’s discovery of chloroform in 1847. In the early nineteenth century, surgery was severely limited by pain: operations had to be performed in under thirty seconds by surgeons like Robert Liston, while conscious patients were held down screaming, frequently dying from sheer cardiovascular shock. Simpson discovered that inhaling chloroform vapor induced reliable, prolonged unconsciousness without the explosive flammability of ether. Although chloroform faced early opposition following the overdose death of Hannah Greener in 1848 and religious objections, Queen Victoria’s public acceptance of it in 1853 established it as standard medical practice. Consequently, surgeons no longer had to rush operations, allowing them to perform careful, precise, and complex procedures, which drastically reduced immediate deaths from operative trauma.<br><br>However, surgery only became truly safe when Joseph Lister conquered the second barrier: wound infection. While anaesthetics eliminated pain, they initially sparked the disastrous 'Black Period' of surgery (1846–1865), because surgeons attempted deeper operations with unwashed hands, causing mortality from hospital gangrene to soar. In 1865, Lister applied Louis Pasteur’s Germ Theory to surgical practice, realizing that sepsis was caused by living airborne microbes. Lister introduced carbolic acid to clean wounds, dressing James Greenlees’ open compound fracture with carbolic lint and spraying a fine carbolic mist over surgical incisions. Lister demonstrated that this antiseptic system reduced his surgical mortality rate at Glasgow Royal Infirmary from 45.7% to 15.0%. Consequently, for the first time in medical history, surgeons possessed an effective chemical weapon to destroy the microscopic pathogens responsible for fatal post-operative sepsis.<br><br>Furthermore, surgical safety was perfected in the late nineteenth century through the transition from antiseptic to aseptic surgery. Surgeons recognized that carbolic acid was caustic to tissue and irritated breathing, shifting their focus from killing germs already inside wounds to excluding all germs from the operating theatre entirely. Charles Chamberland’s 1881 invention of the steam autoclave allowed instruments and dressings to be sterilized with high-pressure steam rather than chemical washes. By the 1890s, operating theatres were lined with scrubbed glazed porcelain tiles, and surgical teams donned boiled cotton gowns, sterilized masks, and rubber gloves. This directly resulted in an operating environment virtually free of bacterial contamination, permanently transforming surgery into a reliable, lifesaving science.",
